<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Orthodox Judaism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Orthodox Jews</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> is the collective term for the traditionalist branches of contemporary Judaism, which hold that the Written and Oral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/torah">Torah</a> were revealed by God on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-sinai">Mount Sinai</a> and faithfully transmitted since. It calls for strict observance of Jewish law, or halakha, interpreted only by traditional methods. Though its beliefs are old, the movement is modern: it arose from the breakdown of the autonomous Jewish community from the late 18th century, in struggle against secularisation and rivals such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reform-judaism">Reform Judaism</a>, and the term Orthodox <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> was first recorded in 1795. It is broadly divided into the Haredi and the Modern Orthodox, and is the largest Jewish religious group.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Modern Judaism includes several movements: <b>Orthodox Judaism</b>, which holds that the Torah and its rabbinic interpretation are divinely given and binding; Reform or Liberal Judaism, which emphasises ethics and adapts practice to modern life; and Conservative or Masorti Judaism between them, as well as many Jews who identify…</blockquote><a href="/religion/judaism">Judaism · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li></ul></section>
